  "body": {
    "comment": "PFMS — Eight Governance Concerns Requiring Clarification Before Approval\n\naraC’don | Independent Cardano DRep | 20 November 2026\n\nTrivolve Tech requests 9,600,000 ADA from the Cardano Treasury to develop the Police Force Management System (PFMS), initially intended for deployment by the government of Telangana, India. Quixy (Vivid Minds Pvt Ltd) is a subcontractor, while the Cardano Foundation is the designated administrator.\n\nBeyond the proposed technical benefits, eight governance concerns require clarification before community resources are committed.\n\n1. Transparency of beneficiaries and previous Treasury funding\n\nThe disclosure covering the previous 24 months concerns Trivolve Tech and Quixy but does not provide the Cardano Foundation's history of Treasury withdrawals.\n\nYet the Cardano Foundation is the designated administrator, and 200,000 ADA are allocated to administration, including administrative expenses and related costs.\n\nWho are the actual economic beneficiaries, how is this allocation distributed, and what disclosure obligations apply to each party? [1]\n\n2. Access to essential contractual provisions\n\nThe agreements involving the government of Telangana are described as confidential and have reportedly been reviewed by the Cardano Foundation.\n\nA separate legal contract between Trivolve Tech and the Cardano Foundation is also envisaged.\n\nHow can DReps assess financial guarantees, liability, recovery mechanisms, and contractual obligations without access to the essential provisions, at least in a suitably redacted form?\n\nConfidentiality should not prevent informed governance decisions. [2]\n\n3. Delegation of effective Treasury oversight\n\nThe community authorizes the withdrawal, but the Cardano Foundation and an independent assurer verify milestone completion and determine whether subsequent payments may be released.\n\nWho resolves disagreements concerning milestone acceptance? Who can suspend payments? What independent remedies remain available to DReps after approval?\n\nAdministrative delegation should not reduce community oversight to a single initial vote. [3]\n\n4. Asymmetry between collective financial risk and private remuneration\n\nAn initial tranche of 1.8 million ADA is scheduled for release upon signing the administrator's contract, before the first technical milestone is accepted.\n\nStaggered disbursements limit future exposure but do not guarantee recovery of funds already paid.\n\nTrivolve Tech receives development funding through the Treasury-financed project, 10% of paid verification revenue, and separate maintenance fees from the state government, shared with its subcontractors as applicable.\n\nMeanwhile, the Treasury receives a proposed 70% share of paid verification revenue, without any guaranteed minimum.\n\nWhat enforceable protections ensure that collective financial exposure is proportionate to the economic benefits and rights received by the Cardano community? [4]\n\n5. Network capacity and shared infrastructure costs\n\nPFMS projects more than 1.5 million annual mainnet transactions, with potential expansion to additional Indian states and other jurisdictions.\n\nAlthough the government would pay transaction fees, these payments do not automatically demonstrate that all network externalities have been assessed.\n\nEven cryptographic fingerprints require transaction processing, block capacity, bandwidth, and storage resources.\n\nWhat independent scalability assessment demonstrates that large-scale expansion will not disproportionately affect other Cardano users, stake pool operators, or infrastructure costs? [5]\n\n6. Constitutional purpose, fundamental rights, and the limits of cryptographic evidence\n\nPFMS proposes an integrated evidence infrastructure connecting surveillance cameras, automatic number plate recognition systems, police records, forensic facilities, prosecution, and courts.\n\nCryptographic integrity does not establish the truthfulness, completeness, or lawful collection of original evidence.\n\nAn immutable record of inaccurate or unlawfully collected information does not make that information accurate or lawful.\n\nWhat independent legal and human-rights assessments have been conducted?\n\nWhat safeguards prevent disproportionate surveillance, institutional misuse, or the creation of an infrastructure that strengthens the traceability of police records without equivalent protections for the individuals concerned?\n\nHow can affected individuals challenge inaccurate evidence and obtain effective remedies?\n\nThe use of local software agents does not, by itself, establish autonomous AI decision-making. Any AI-specific risks should be assessed against the actual system architecture.\n\nThe constitutional question is not merely whether Cardano can support this infrastructure, but whether financing this particular application is consistent with the community's principles and responsibilities. [6]\n\n7. Projected government-scale adoption versus enforceable deliverables\n\nThe proposal emphasizes state-scale adoption, approximately 5.1 million decentralized identifiers, and more than 1.5 million projected annual transactions.\n\nHowever, the final milestone M4 requires at least 390,000 transactions over three months and explicitly concerns the first participating districts, not full statewide deployment.\n\nThe community may therefore approve funding based on government-scale ambitions while the final payment conditions require only partial deployment.\n\nWhich projected benefits are binding deliverables, and which remain long-term expectations?\n\nThese distinctions should be explicit before the vote. [7]\n\n8. Incomplete economic disclosure and reputational exposure\n\nThe proposed revenue allocation — 70% to the Cardano Treasury, 20% to core Cardano library maintenance, and 10% to Trivolve Tech — applies to the optional paid verification service.\n\nThe proposal does not establish a guaranteed volume of paid verifications or a minimum financial return to the Treasury.\n\nFurthermore, the value and duration of the separate maintenance agreements are not fully disclosed.\n\nTechnical milestones could therefore be completed without generating significant verification revenue for Cardano.\n\nThe open-source software may provide genuine ecosystem value, but this should be distinguished from measurable financial returns.\n\nFinancing large-scale policing infrastructure also raises reputational questions for an ecosystem built around decentralization, individual responsibility, and community governance.\n\nThese risks deserve independent assessment before public Treasury resources are committed. [8]\n\n---\n\nConclusion — Who Bears the Risk, and Who Controls the Benefits?\n\nWho funds the project? Who makes the decisions? Who controls the disbursements? Who bears the losses? Who receives the revenue?\n\nTechnical performance, constitutional compliance, financial accountability, and fundamental rights are separate requirements. None can be assumed from the others.\n\nThe Cardano Treasury is being asked to commit 9.6 million ADA to a project combining public infrastructure, private commercial interests, and institutional oversight.\n\nMilestone-based funding limits future exposure but does not guarantee recovery of capital already committed.\n\nLikewise, alignment with Cardano 2030 strategic objectives does not, by itself, establish that a particular application deserves Treasury financing.\n\nBefore approval, the community should receive independently verifiable information concerning essential contractual safeguards, economic arrangements, administrator responsibilities, fund recovery mechanisms, network impact, and fundamental-rights protections.\n\nThe burden of demonstrating these safeguards rests with those requesting Treasury funding.\n\nSecuring evidence does not establish the truth of its contents.\n\nSecuring is not controlling.\n\n— araC’don\\\nIndependent Cardano DRep\n\n---\n\nDocumentary References\n\n[1] TRIVOLVE TECH, PFMS: Bringing Government Scale Adoption to Cardano by Trivolve Tech, Section 7.2, “Budget Allocation”; Section 9, Article II §§7.2, 7.4 and 7.5.\n\n[2] TRIVOLVE TECH, PFMS: Bringing Government Scale Adoption to Cardano by Trivolve Tech, Section 8, “Risk Management — Adoption”; Section 10, “Administration”.\n\n[3] TRIVOLVE TECH, Work Packages, p. 6, “Milestones and Acceptance — The Gate”; PFMS: Bringing Government Scale Adoption to Cardano by Trivolve Tech, Section 9, Article II §§7.5–7.6.\n\n[4] TRIVOLVE TECH, PFMS: Bringing Government Scale Adoption to Cardano by Trivolve Tech, Section 3, “Revenue Streams and Reasoning for a Treasury Withdrawal”; “Revenue Beyond Transactions”; Section 8, “Build Failure Risk”; Work Packages, p. 6, Milestones M0–M4.\n\n[5] TRIVOLVE TECH, PFMS: Bringing Government Scale Adoption to Cardano by Trivolve Tech, Section 4, “Projected Impact on Cardano Mainnet Transaction Count”; Section 5, “Cardano 2030 KPI Alignment”; Work Packages, p. 8, Milestone M4.\n\n[6] TRIVOLVE TECH, PFMS: Bringing Government Scale Adoption to Cardano by Trivolve Tech, Section 2, “The Components that Make PFMS Work”; Work Packages, p. 5, WP7, “Five Stage Modules”; CARDANO, Cardano Blockchain Ecosystem Constitution.\n\n[7] TRIVOLVE TECH, Proposal Summary Page, p. 1, “Value Proposition” and “Deliverables”; Work Packages, pp. 6–8, Milestones M3–M4.\n\n[8] TRIVOLVE TECH, PFMS: Bringing Government Scale Adoption to Cardano by Trivolve Tech, Section 3, “Revenue Streams and Reasoning for a Treasury Withdrawal”; “Revenue Beyond Transactions”; Work Packages, pp. 6–8, “Milestones and Acceptance”.\n\nSource Documents\n\nOfficial Governance Action — GovTool:\\\n[https://gov.tools/governance_actions/72491608dc1a500d38613214069a2bd3b7e9a0e670d5abd7d6374e6bb54fc0e8](https://gov.tools/governance_actions/72491608dc1a500d38613214069a2bd3b7e9a0e670d5abd7d6374e6bb54fc0e8)\n\nTRIVOLVE TECH — Full PFMS Proposal:\\\n[https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmWaohWccfdSe9NQz5DQ5myZ9QHWg61xhRvN8KTJGiTGVF](https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmWaohWccfdSe9NQz5DQ5myZ9QHWg61xhRvN8KTJGiTGVF)\n\nTRIVOLVE TECH — Work Pack"
  },
  "hashAlgorithm": "blake2b-256"
